 "pieces": [
  {
   "seg": "N0",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 0.6,
   "t1": 2.922,
   "vo": "Ramesh is a story. These are not."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A0",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 8.6,
   "t1": 15.798,
   "vo": "Every blow next door travels through the ground into old buildings like Ramesh's. Cracks grow, but nobody keeps an honest record."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A1",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 16.9,
   "t1": 27.628,
   "vo": "This is TremorMesh. Every flat keeps one phone on the floor. Together, they record every tremor, and nobody can rewrite that record. Not even us."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A2a",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 28.6,
   "t1": 36.541,
   "vo": "The phone lies still and listens. It reacts only when the floor really shakes. Pick it up, and that reading doesn't count."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A2b",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 38.6,
   "t1": 45.659,
   "vo": "One phone proves nothing, and one man is easy to dismiss. A tremor counts only when three flats feel it at the same moment."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A2c",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 46.7,
   "t1": 52.691,
   "vo": "The building committee invites each phone. Every phone gets a public name, and the rules are public too."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A2d",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 54.1,
   "t1": 60.88,
   "vo": "Each phone signs what it felt, like a signature on a letter. Change one number, and everyone can see it."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A2e",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 62.0,
   "t1": 76.721,
   "vo": "Under the hood: every reading is signed and stored on Arkiv, a public blockchain database. Names and rules live on E N S. Sealed evidence sits on Swarm. And your own browser does the counting, not our servers."
  },
  {
   "seg": "A3",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 77.817,
   "t1": 91.749,
   "vo": "Here's the real thing, at ETHGlobal Mumbai. Four phones, one table. Of a hundred and forty-one readings, a hundred and thirty-seven were thrown out. One real event got through, at twenty-three hundred hours, and it was sealed for good."
  },
  {
   "seg": "B1",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 92.95,
   "t1": 101.216,
   "vo": "But the night the bed shook, it wasn't construction. When the internet goes down, the same phones become a rescue network."
  },
  {
   "seg": "B2a",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 102.15,
   "t1": 107.537,
   "vo": "When enough phones feel a strong quake at once, they switch to disaster mode."
  },
  {
   "seg": "B2b",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 108.433,
   "t1": 120.972,
   "vo": "With no internet, they connect directly over Bluetooth and Wi-Fi, forming a mesh. Some phones become hubs, linked to many others. Some become connectors, bridging groups, so every message finds a way through."
  },
  {
   "seg": "B2c",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 121.867,
   "t1": 131.712,
   "vo": "Every link also shows how close two phones are, giving a map of who is near whom. If a phone goes quiet under the rubble, its last neighbours show rescuers where to look."
  },
  {
   "seg": "B3",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 133.867,
   "t1": 145.941,
   "vo": "Here's how our app works with no internet. One phone taps, I need help, and it hops through the mesh to everyone. The list shows who's safe, who needs help, and who hasn't answered."
  },
  {
   "seg": "Z",
   "k": 0,
   "t0": 146.967,
   "t1": 153.84,
   "vo": "Before disaster, proof no one can rewrite. After it, a way to find each other. TremorMesh."
  }
 ],
